        <p>I'm pleased to introduce <a href="https://breakfastrunning.club/">Breakfast</a>, a running analysis app for iPhone and iPad.</p>
<p>Breakfast syncs your data from Apple Health, providing a runner-minded view of your performance and a variety of tools to help with your training. It's <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/breakfast-running-performance/id6790165362">available in the App Store for free</a>.</p>
<p>I got into running in 2021, shortly after the birth of our first child. It was the first exercise activity that really stuck for me. Analysing and monitoring the data was a really helpful part of building that habit.</p>
<p>Unfortunately, many of the apps out there require accounts, make poor use of the latest OS features, and offer inconsistent or frustrating monetisation models. With Breakfast, I've sought to create the right tool for me, first and foremost.</p>
<p>I've been working on the app for much of 2026 and using it full-time for my running analysis for several months. After honing it throughout the year, I've decided to publish it for others to enjoy.</p>
<p>Breakfast does not require a user account, nor does it have an in-app social network. There's zero tracking or telemetry.</p>
<p>You simply enable read-only access to various metrics in Health and record your runs as you normally would, using Workout on watchOS, Runna, Strava, or otherwise. As long as your runs sync with Health, you can use Breakfast. (But it's best if you train with an Apple Watch.)</p>
<p>The initial version is focused on analysis, comparison, and tracking your progress. The core functionality:</p>
<ul>
<li>Analysis:
<ul>
<li>Breakfast is, first and foremost, a tool for analysing your running. You can view a heat map of your run, analyse pace (including grade-adjusted estimates), review your heart rate and elevation history, assess your running dynamics and splits, and more.</li>
<li>Breakfast also identifies similar routes, allowing you to compare runs over time.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>Personal Bests:
<ul>
<li>Breakfast keeps track of your personal bests, showing your times across distances from 100 metres up to a full marathon.</li>
<li>You can review your top 10 times for each distance and choose to race your personal bests, syncing a pacer workout to your Apple Watch.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>Goals:
<ul>
<li>Set distance, frequency, and time goals to help keep your training on track.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>Shoes:
<ul>
<li>Tag your runs with your running shoes and keep up with wear and tear. You can set mileage targets and receive a notification when your shoes reach them.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>Trends:
<ul>
<li>Taking the analysis a step further, Trends lets you explore changes in metrics such as VO₂ max, pace, heart rate, and load balance.</li>
<li>You can also view estimates of your potential race times and your performance on the routes you run most often.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>Sharing:
<ul>
<li>Share summaries of your latest runs, PBs, and activity over various periods of time. You can even add breakfast food stickers for a bit of fun.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>Misc.:
<ul>
<li>Initial support for App Intents, Shortcuts, and Siri is included at launch.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<p>Breakfast is built with iOS and iPadOS 27 in mind, including full support for the iPhone Duo. It is available in both US and British English at launch.</p>
<p>It's built using modern Swift conventions and, yes, has been significantly supported by AI development tools. I'm an amateur developer, but I've been a student of the indie Apple ecosystem for 15+ years. Much like <a href="https://lexontech.org/claude-writes-code-i-author-the-apps">Lex</a>, I've brought that perspective to the process to create something useful, modern, and lightweight. I'm proud of the result.</p>
<p>Breakfast has a comprehensive roadmap for the future, spanning small features (e.g., adding support for PBs beyond 26.2 miles) to large (e.g., adaptable training programs). My primary objective is for Breakfast to fully replace bloated and poorly maintained apps like Runna for many. For now, it's here as an insight-packed companion to your other tools, but with a lot of ambition under the surface.</p>
<p>You can <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/breakfast-running-performance/id6790165362">download Breakfast for free from the App Store</a>, or take a closer look at <a href="https://breakfastrunning.club/">breakfastrunning.club</a>.</p>
<p>Breakfast also has a sibling: <a href="https://one37.net/daybreak/">Daybreak</a>, which quietly launched on November 14. It exports your Apple Health history as readable files for your own tools. I'll share more about that tomorrow.</p>

      